---
// Case study for rust-evm-indexer.
//
// Source: docs/integrity-fixes-record.md in that repository, checked against the
// commits (554b6b5, 327fd40, f44a694 .. 8c64933) and fact-checked by the session that
// made the fixes. Do not claim "10M+ transactions" or latency figures: neither is
// backed by anything checked in. The migration is tested only on a copy of the old schema.
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";

const REPO = "https://github.com/Nihal-Pandey-2302/rust-evm-indexer";

// rust-evm-indexer src/db.rs on integrity-fixes, verbatim
const ROLLBACK = `pub async fn rollback_to(pool: &PgPool, keep_height: u64) -> Result<(), sqlx::Error> {
    let height = keep_height as i64;
    let mut tx = pool.begin().await?;
    sqlx::query("DELETE FROM logs WHERE block_number > $1")
        .bind(height)
        .execute(&mut *tx)
        .await?;
    sqlx::query("DELETE FROM transactions WHERE block_number > $1")
        .bind(height)
        .execute(&mut *tx)
        .await?;
    sqlx::query("DELETE FROM blocks WHERE block_number > $1")
        .bind(height)
        .execute(&mut *tx)
        .await?;
    sqlx::query("UPDATE indexer_status SET last_processed_block = $2 WHERE indexer_name = $1")
        .bind(INDEXER_NAME)
        .bind(height)
        .execute(&mut *tx)
        .await?;
    tx.commit().await
}`;

// What each test showed on 1741604, the commit before the fixes
const TESTS = [
  ["one_block_reorg_reingests_replaced_block", "checkpoint 103, expected 102: the replaced block is never fetched again"],
  ["two_block_reorg_rolls_back_to_fork_point", "checkpoint 103, expected 101: a stale block from the old fork stays"],
  ["failed_rollback_leaves_data_and_checkpoint_unchanged", "6 transactions and 12 logs left, expected 8 and 16"],
  ["reorg_deeper_than_limit_is_not_rolled_back", "no error: one block rolled back regardless of depth"],
  ["failed_receipt_does_not_commit_block", "block committed with a transaction that has no status and no logs"],
  ["missing_receipt_does_not_commit_block", "same, for a receipt the node returns as null"],
  ["receipt_from_another_block_does_not_commit_block", "a receipt from the other fork stored with its logs"],
  ["reapplying_a_block_keeps_one_copy_of_each_log", "16 logs, expected 8"],
  ["second_block_at_same_height_is_rejected", "the second block was inserted"],
  ["block_missing_from_rpc_does_not_skip_height", "checkpoint 102 with no block 101"],
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="Re-reading a crash-safe indexer"
  dek="An Ethereum indexer that writes each block and its checkpoint in one Postgres transaction, designed so a crash cannot leave half a block. That part held up. Re-reading the rest found three ways it could still lose or duplicate data, and making it testable turned up two more."
  source={{ label: "The repository on GitHub", url: REPO }}
  meta={[
    { text: "Nihal-Pandey-2302/rust-evm-indexer", href: REPO },
    { text: "the fix commits", href: `${REPO}/compare/30932cf...8c64933` },
    { text: "Rust, Tokio, SQLx, PostgreSQL" },
    { text: "10 tests fail on the old code; all 11 pass after the fixes" },
  ]}
>
  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>The question</h2>
    <p>
      An indexer copies blocks, transactions and logs from an Ethereum node into Postgres. The question that decides
      whether it can be trusted: if the process dies halfway through a block, what is in the database when it comes
      back?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>The first design</h2>
    <p>
      The first version, in May 2025, wrote each row straight to the pool, with no transaction around a block and no
      record of where it had got to. The log insert already carried a note to myself:
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <div class="cs-panel cs-term"><span class="is-dim">// Relies on 'id' BIGSERIAL for conflict. Consider unique constraint on (tx_hash, log_index_in_tx) for content-based idempotency.</span></div>
      <figcaption>
        From the log insert in the first version
        (<a href={`${REPO}/commit/554b6b5`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link">554b6b5</a>).
        It took eighteen months to act on.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>The part that held up</h2>
    <p>
      Two days later
      (<a href={`${REPO}/commit/327fd40`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link">327fd40</a>)
      every block was written in one Postgres transaction, with the sync checkpoint inside it: the block,
      its transactions, their logs, then the checkpoint, then commit. A crash before the commit leaves nothing behind. A
      crash after it leaves a complete block and a checkpoint at that block, so the next run starts after it and the
      block is never processed twice. On the later re-read, this part still held.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>Re-reading the rest</h2>
    <p>
      A re-read in November 2026, made to check what this site could honestly say about the project, went through the
      rest. The README claimed reorg handling and idempotent writes. Three things did not hold.
    </p>

    <h3>1. A reorg left a hole</h3>
    <p>
      When a new block's parent did not match the stored block below it, the code deleted that stored block and
      everything above it, in three separate statements, and left the checkpoint where it was.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <dl class="cs-panel cs-term cs-kv">
        <div><dt>stored</dt><dd>100  101  102  103</dd></div>
        <div><dt>block 104 arrives</dt><dd>its parent is not the stored 103</dd></div>
        <div><dt>old rollback</dt><dd class="is-bad">deletes 103, checkpoint stays at 103, next cycle fetches 104: the new 103 is never fetched</dd></div>
        <div><dt>fixed rollback</dt><dd class="is-good">walks back to 102, the last height that matches the chain, deletes above it and rewinds the checkpoint to 102 in one transaction: 103 is fetched again</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <figcaption>The one-block case. The old code also only ever looked one block back, and a crash between its deletes left logs gone with their transactions still in place.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>2. A failed receipt committed an incomplete block</h3>
    <p>
      Receipts carry a transaction's status and logs. If fetching one still failed after three tries, the error was
      turned into "no receipt": the transaction was written with no status and no logs, and the checkpoint moved on. The
      block was never revisited, which is exactly the state the README said could not happen.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Logs were not idempotent</h3>
    <p>
      Blocks and transactions ignored duplicates on insert. Logs had nothing to conflict on, the note from the first
      version still unacted on. Only the checkpoint kept them from being written twice, so any path that re-applied a
      block would have duplicated every log.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>Making it testable first</h2>
    <p>
      The repository had no tests. Before changing anything, the ingester moved behind a small trait for the chain, so a
      stub node could serve blocks, forks and failures on demand, with a real Postgres underneath. Eight tests were
      written against the old behaviour first, and every one failed. Two more, added while fixing, also fail on the old
      code.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>The fixes</h2>
    <p>
      A reorg now walks back from the stored tip until the stored hash matches the chain, up to 64 blocks. Then one
      transaction deletes everything above that height and rewinds the checkpoint to it. A reorg deeper than 64 blocks
      stops ingestion and changes nothing, rather than guessing.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <div class="cs-panel cs-code"><Code code={ROLLBACK} lang="rust" theme="css-variables" /></div>
      <figcaption>
        The rollback, from src/db.rs
        (<a href={`${REPO}/commit/df82957`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link">df82957</a>).
        The checkpoint rewind is in the same transaction as the deletes.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      A receipt that fails, comes back empty, or belongs to a different block now fails the whole block before its
      transaction starts, so the checkpoint stays put and the block is retried. Logs gained a key, and checking what to
      key them on turned up a mislabelled column: the stored index was the log's position in the block, not in its
      transaction. They are now unique on block hash and log index, and a migration for existing databases, tested on a copy of the
      old schema, adds the key and removes duplicates.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>Two more, found while writing the tests</h2>
    <p>
      If the node did not return a block yet (a lagging node), the old loop skipped that height, committed the next one
      and moved the checkpoint past the gap. And a receipt fetched just after the node itself reorged could belong to the
      other fork, and was stored with its logs. Both now fail the block instead.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>What the tests showed</h2>
    <p>Run against the commit before the fixes, then after:</p>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <dl class="cs-panel cs-term cs-kv cs-kv-wide">
        {TESTS.map(([name, before]) => (
          <div><dt>{name}</dt><dd class="is-bad">{before}</dd></div>
        ))}
      </dl>
      <figcaption>What each test found on the old code. With the fixes, all 11 pass, including a test of the migration, which is new code with no "before".</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>Who did what</h2>
    <p>
      The first design and the per-block transaction with its checkpoint are mine. The November re-read, the five fixes
      and the tests were done with Claude, as every
      <a href={`${REPO}/compare/30932cf...8c64933`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link">fix commit</a>
      records.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>What is still open</h2>
    <ul class="cs-list">
      <li>A reorg is noticed only when the next block arrives. If the new chain is shorter than what is stored, the stale tip stays until the chain grows past it.</li>
      <li>A reorg deeper than 64 blocks needs a manual rollback; the repository has the statement.</li>
      <li>The tests run against a stub node, not the real HTTP client.</li>
      <li>A process crash in the middle of a block write is not tested directly. It rests on Postgres transactions; the injected mid-rollback failure is the closest test.</li>
      <li>Databases written by the old code may still hold gaps. A check script finds them; the migration does not repair them.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>

